          "heading": "Sectors of Economic Activities",
          "para": "Economic activities are grouped into three sectors according to the nature of what is produced. The primary sector produces goods by exploiting natural resources: growing crops and rearing animals in agriculture, dairy farming, catching fish, cutting timber in forestry and digging minerals in mining - the product here is a natural product, which is why this sector is also called the agriculture and related sector. The secondary sector covers activities in which natural products are changed into other forms through methods of manufacturing: cotton becomes cloth, sugarcane becomes sugar, iron ore becomes steel, milk becomes ghee. Since this sector is associated with factories and industry, it is called the industrial sector. The tertiary sector generates services that support the other two and cannot be stored: transport, storage, trade, banking, communication, insurance, education, health, tourism and information technology. The same loaf of bread shows all three: a farmer grew the wheat, a mill ground it into flour, and a transporter and shopkeeper carried and sold it.",
