          "heading": "Comparing the Three Sectors: GDP",
          "para": "We compare sectors by comparing the value of what they produce. Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is defined as the value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a particular year; it shows how big the economy is. Only final goods are counted. Final goods are those that reach the consumer directly, while intermediate goods - wheat bought by a flour mill, yarn bought by a weaver - are used up in producing some other good. The value of the final good already includes the value of all intermediate goods used in it, so adding both would count everything twice, a mistake called double counting that would falsely inflate GDP. In India the task of measuring GDP is undertaken by the Central Government through its statistics ministry, which collects data from every state and every sector. The resulting sectoral shares tell the structure of the economy: today the tertiary sector contributes more than half of India's GDP, the secondary sector roughly a quarter, and the primary sector the remainder.",
