          "heading": "Employment vs GDP: The Indian Paradox",
          "para": "Here lies the central paradox of Indian development. The primary sector now produces only a small fraction of GDP - less than one-fifth - yet it continues to employ more than half of India's total workforce, far more than any other sector. The tertiary sector, which contributes the most value, employs far fewer people; the secondary sector sits in between on both counts. Workers remain tied to agriculture because the rest of the economy has not created enough decent jobs for them, and because many lack the education, skills or urban contacts that industry and services demand. Others cannot leave the land since it is their only asset and security. The consequence is underemployment: people are working, but at far below their capacity, so the economy loses the output they could have produced. Every underemployed farm hand is potential labour for a factory or service that does not exist. Closing this gap between where Indians work and where value is created is the central employment challenge, and it explains the policy responses discussed in the coming sections.",
