      "summary": "This chapter explains why money exists and how modern credit works. It begins with the barter system and the double coincidence of wants that money removes, then describes modern currency - fiat notes issued by the Reserve Bank of India and coins issued by the Government of India - and demand deposits, which are used through cheques. It shows how banks keep only about 15 per cent of deposits as cash reserve and lend the rest, earning from the spread between deposit and loan interest, and how they mediate between depositors and borrowers. Through the contrasting stories of Salim and Swapna, it demonstrates that credit can either support production or push a borrower into a debt trap. The four terms of credit - interest rate, collateral, documentation and mode of repayment - are defined, and formal sources (banks and cooperatives supervised by the RBI) are contrasted with informal moneylenders and traders, on whom poor households depend at high cost. Finally, it explains Self Help Groups of 15-20 members, mostly poor women, whose savings and group responsibility win them collateral-free bank loans, making cheap credit and empowerment possible together.",
