          "heading": "Modern Forms of Money: Currency and Deposits",
          "para": "Modern money takes two broad forms: currency and deposits with banks. Currency consists of paper notes and coins. Unlike old metallic money, it has no intrinsic value: the paper and metal in a hundred-rupee note are worth a tiny fraction of its face value. It is accepted because it is fiat money - an order of the government that all must accept in payment. In India, the Reserve Bank of India issues currency notes on behalf of the Central Government, and the law permits no other individual or organisation to issue currency; coins are issued by the Government of India. Acceptance rests on two pillars: legal authority - rupees are legal tender for settling every debt - and trust, since each person believes everyone else will also accept the note. The second form, demand deposits with banks, behaves like money because it can be withdrawn or transferred on demand and used through cheques; together with currency it forms the money supply of a modern economy.",
