          "para": "The other pillar of modern money is the bank deposit. People keep their spare cash with banks because deposits are safe, earn some interest, and can be taken out whenever needed; since the bank must return the money on demand, such deposits are called demand deposits. Demand deposits share the essential features of money because they can be used to settle payments without cash: the account holder writes a cheque, which is an instruction to the bank to pay a particular amount from the writer's account to the person named on it. Salaries can be credited directly, traders can pay suppliers across the country, and modern instruments - debit cards, UPI transfers, internet banking - are all ways of moving demand deposits; notes and coins enter the picture only when someone withdraws cash. Because deposits can be commanded to pay like currency, they are treated as money, and the total money in use in the economy is currency plus demand deposits.",
