          "heading": "Two Different Credit Situations: Salim and Swapna",
          "para": "Whether credit helps or harms depends on the borrower's circumstances, which the textbook shows through two contrasting stories. Salim, a shoe manufacturer, visits a large retailer and wins an order on the strength of an advance payment; that advance is credit which lets him buy leather, thread and glue, complete production on schedule, deliver the shoes and keep the profit - credit here meets working capital needs and expands income. Swapna, a small farmer, takes a cultivation loan at a high rate of interest to buy seeds, fertilisers and pesticides; when pests destroy her crop, her expected income disappears but the loan survives. To repay, she borrows again at heavier cost and finally sells part of her land - a debt trap in which each new loan exists only to repay the old one while assets drain away. The lesson: credit is productive when returns are certain and interest is low, as for Salim; it is destructive when risk is high and lenders are costly, as for Swapna.",
