          "para": "A Self Help Group (SHG) solves the collateral problem by substituting group trust for individual property. Typically 15 to 20 members - most often poor women from one neighbourhood - meet regularly and each saves a small fixed sum, building a common fund from the thrift. Out of this pool, members can take small loans at a reasonable rate of interest without any collateral, for consumption needs or for starting tiny income-generating activities: tailoring, making pickles, papad or soap, goat or poultry rearing, running a small shop. The group itself decides the purpose, the rate and the repayment schedule, and repayment discipline stays high because every member's behaviour is watched by the others. After a year or two of regular saving, the group becomes eligible for a bank loan in the group's own name, again without collateral, which it on-lends to members - the SHG-bank linkage programme. Beyond credit, SHGs make women financially self-reliant and give them a platform to discuss health, nutrition, education and social issues, so the group is an instrument of both finance and empowerment.",
