      "summary": "This chapter explains how economies have knit together through multinational corporations, foreign trade and technology. It defines MNCs and shows, through Cargill Foods' buyout of Parakh Foods and the Ford Motors-Mahindra joint venture of 1995, how they set up or link production across countries to cut costs and reach markets. Foreign trade - illustrated by cheap Chinese toys entering Indian markets - integrates markets, expands consumer choice and sharpens competition for producers. Globalisation is driven by rapid improvements in transport and communication technology (container shipping, air cargo, internet, call centres and BPO) and by liberalisation, the removal of trade barriers such as tariffs and quotas after 1991, a process now framed by WTO rules that India and other developing members criticise when rich countries subsidise their own agriculture. The chapter weighs the winners - MNC investment, IT and BPO jobs, globalising Indian companies like Tata Motors, Infosys and Ranbaxy, and consumers - against struggling small producers in toys, tyres and vegetable oil and insecure casual workers, and closes with the case for fair globalisation supported by government action at home and at the WTO.",
