          "heading": "Foreign Trade and Integration of Markets",
          "para": "Foreign trade is the oldest bridge between markets. The basic reason for trade is simple: no country produces everything its people want, and trade lets consumers buy goods made anywhere, far beyond what domestic producers alone offer. The classic case is Chinese toys entering Indian markets: shops filled with cheaper, brightly finished toys, and consumers gained greater variety at lower prices - a direct gain in choice. But the same flood hit Indian toy makers, who lost customers, cut production and shed workers - competition is the other face of trade. As trade grows, the markets of different countries become increasingly connected: prices, demand and production decisions in one market begin to move with events in another, so what happens to toys in one country matters to sellers in another. This growing interconnection is called the integration of markets, and it is the everyday, visible face of globalisation.",
