          "heading": "Globalisation: Meaning and Drivers",
          "para": "Globalisation is the process of rapid integration or interconnection among the markets of different countries, as goods, services, investment, technology and people move across borders faster than ever. Two forces drive it. The first is technology. In transport, container services, larger cargo ships, air cargo and better packaging have slashed the time and cost of moving goods across oceans, so production can be split among countries and still reach markets quickly. In information and communication, telephones, fax, the internet and mobile networks have made services themselves tradable: a call centre in India answers queries from abroad, software is maintained across continents, and business processes are outsourced to countries like India - the BPO boom - because skill can be delivered by cable at low cost. The second force is policy: liberalisation means the removal of barriers or restrictions set by the government on foreign trade and investment, so goods can be imported and exported freely and foreign companies can invest more easily. India liberalised after 1991; technology opened the door and liberalisation walked the world through it.",
