          "heading": "The Consumer in the Marketplace",
          "para": "Every one of us is a consumer, but the marketplace does not always treat the buyer fairly. Sellers have many ways of exploiting an unwary customer: adulterated food and edible oils, defective or substandard goods, underweight and false measures, inflated prices and hidden charges. The two sides of the market are also unequal in power: producers and traders are few, organised and well informed, while consumers are many, scattered and individually weak, each knowing only what the label - or the salesman - reveals. Hence the familiar image of the 'consumer as king' is largely a myth: in practice the buyer has incomplete information, no way to test quality before purchase, and little time, money or legal knowledge to fight back after a bad one. This unequal situation, repeated in every market from vegetables to medicines, is the starting point of the chapter: it explains why consumer protection through laws, councils, courts and, above all, an alert and informed consumer became necessary in the first place.",
