          "para": "COPRA gives consumers several specific rights, beginning with safety and information. The right to safety protects the consumer from goods and services that are hazardous to life and property - a pressure cooker with a faulty safety valve, an electrical appliance without proper insulation, a medicine that has not been properly tested. The right to information obliges the seller to disclose everything a buyer needs to decide: ingredients, directions for use, batch number, date of manufacture and expiry, price, quantity, the manufacturer's address and necessary warnings; nowhere is this more vital than in medicines and packaged food. This right has been extended to government functioning by the Right to Information Act, 2005, under which any citizen can demand information from government departments - for example, about the quality of food served in school meals or the grain stocked in ration shops. Knowledge is the consumer's first defence: hazardous goods and hidden facts are both defeated by it.",
